    "lawCode": "Add §169, amd §214, Fam Ct Act; amd §10.40, CP L; amd §§240 & 252, Dom Rel L; amd §212, Judy L",
    "lawSection": "Family Court Act",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S790B\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the judiciary law, the family court\nact, the domestic relations law and the criminal procedure law, in\nrelation to the translation of orders of protection and temporary\norders of protection\n\n PURPOSE OF BILL :\n\nTo require the Office of Court Administration to provide written\ntranslation of orders of protection and temporary orders of protection\nin family court and state supreme court.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 contains a technical change for re-lettering of a section.\n\nSection 2 requires the Office of Court Administration to provide\nwritten translation for orders of protection and temporary orders of\nprotection, where a party to the proceeding has limited English\nproficiency or a limited ability to read English. Written translation\nmust be provided for the top ten most spoken languages in the courts\nin each of the four judicial departments over a 3-year phase-in. OCA\nmust also issue a report on the availability of translation services,\nbroken down by language and department, and submit a second report on\nany technical and operational issues involves with written translation\nservices.\n\nFurther, OCA must establish a pilot program to provide written\ntranslation services in a town or village court within each judicial\ndistrict. A second pilot program must be developed by OCA to provide\nwritten translation in one county in the city of New York and in two\ncounties outside of the city of New York in a state-paid criminal\ncourt.\n\nSections 3, 4 and 5 require written translation for orders of\nprotection to be provided in family court and in state supreme court.\n\nSections 6 and 7 contain technical changes for re-lettering of\nsections.\n\nSection 8 allows the chief administrator of the courts to promulgate\nrules and regulations to effectuate the provisions of this Act.\n\nSection 9 states that the Act takes effect 90 days after becoming law.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nUnder rulemaking by the Office of Court Administration (OCA), parties\nto an order of protection in family court or state supreme court can\ncurrently receive a court appointed interpreter. However, there is no\nrequirement that a written translation in a non-English language of\nthe court order of protected be provided to the parties to the\nproceeding. The Office of Court Administration is currently engaged in\na pilot program to provide written translation for orders of\nprotection for Spanish, Russian and Mandarin speakers. This bill,\nwritten in consultation with OCA, would require OCA to provide written\ntranslation for orders of protection for the top ten most spoken\nlanguages in each of the four judicial departments in the state, over\na three-year phase in. This would greatly benefit parties to a\nproceeding for an order of protection who are non-English speakers,\nenabling them to obtain a copy of the order of protection in a\nlanguage they can read and understand.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\n1998: A 1019 Judiciary\n2001/02: A.7103\n2003/04: A.5364\n2005/06: A.4009\n2007/08: A.4605A Referred to Judiciary\n2015/16: Referred to Judiciary\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone. The Office of Court Administration believes it can accomplish\nthe requirements of this legislation within existing resources.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect 90 days after it shall have become a law\nand shall apply to orders of protection issued on or after such date.\n",
